# Query Performance and Mandatory N+1 Prevention

## Non-negotiable
- Never introduce, preserve, or approve an N+1 query. Never run queries, lazy-loaded relations, or database-touching aggregates/accessors/service methods inside a loop — in controllers, services, actions, jobs, commands, Resources, notifications, exports, or templates.
- Preload every relationship (and nested relationship) a loop or its called code uses: `with()`, `load()`, `loadMissing()`, `withCount()`/`withExists()`/`withSum()`, subqueries, joins, or batch lookups. Polymorphic: `morphWith()`/`loadMorph()`. Recursive data: bounded eager loading or CTEs — never one query per node.
- API Resources must not trigger queries — use `whenLoaded()`, `whenCounted()`, precomputed attributes. Accessors, casts, policies, and appended attributes must not silently query per model.
- Enable the runtime guard: `Model::preventLazyLoading()` (dev/tests) makes a lazy load throw instead of silently costing a query — run the `harden-runtime` skill to stage it safely. Prove boundedness with `DB::enableQueryLog()` at 1 row vs 10 rows; the counts must be equal. Never fix an N+1 by loading an unbounded dataset into memory, changing business behavior, weakening authorization, or hiding bad queries behind caching.

## Query construction
Select only needed columns (keep the primary/foreign keys relationships require); `exists()` for existence checks; database aggregates for counts/sums; paginate unbounded user-facing collections; `chunkById()`/`lazyById()`/queue batches for large background work; confirm indexes on frequent filters, joins, tenant keys, and unique invariants.

## Verification before completion
Search modified paths for queries or relation access inside loops; inspect Resources, accessors, and policies used by collections; verify nested eager loads; the query count must stay bounded as result size grows (compare one record vs many); add a query-count regression test on high-risk paths; report the final strategy and trade-offs.
